@inject IConfiguration Configuration
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy Policy";
    var contactEmail = Configuration["Site:ContactEmail"];
}

<article class="container legal">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="legal-updated">Last updated: 17 October 2026</p>

    <p>
        PhotoWorld is a community for people who love photography. This policy explains what personal
        information we collect when you use PhotoWorld, why, where it is kept and the choices you have.
    </p>

    <h2>What we collect</h2>
    <h3>When you sign in with Google</h3>
    <p>
        You join and sign in to PhotoWorld with your Google account. With your consent, Google shares with us:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>your name,</li>
        <li>your email address (only if Google has verified it),</li>
        <li>your profile picture,</li>
        <li>a unique Google account identifier.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        We never see your Google password, we don't ask for access to your Gmail, contacts, Drive or any other
        Google data, and we never post anything on your behalf.
    </p>

    <h3>Account activity</h3>
    <p>We record when your account was created and when you last signed in.</p>

    <h3>Technical logs</h3>
    <p>
        To keep PhotoWorld running and secure, our servers write technical logs: for example, the page or API
        address requested, error details, and sign-in events with your PhotoWorld user ID. Logs don't contain
        your password or Google sign-in tokens.
    </p>

    <h3>Photos you share</h3>
    <p>
        Photos you upload, with their titles, descriptions, likes and comments, are visible to other signed-in
        PhotoWorld members, unless you choose to feature a photo on the home page (see below). We read the camera settings stored in your photo (camera, lens, focal length,
        aperture, shutter speed, ISO and the date it was taken) and show them on the photo page.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you tick <strong>Show on the PhotoWorld home page</strong> for a photo, that photo, its title and your
        name appear in the slideshow on our public home page, where anyone can see them without signing in.
        It's off by default and you can turn it off at any time by editing the photo.
    </p>
    <p>
        We never store your original file. We save a resized copy with all hidden metadata removed, so the
        location where a photo was taken is never kept or shared. The image files are served from a public
        address: anyone who is given the direct link to an image can view it, even without signing in.
    </p>

    <h3>Blog posts and comments</h3>
    <p>
        Blog posts you publish, and comments you write on posts, are visible to other signed-in members.
        Drafts are visible only to you. Photos in your posts come from your own gallery.
    </p>

    <h3>Places you've been</h3>
    <p>
        On <strong>My places</strong> you can pin places you've visited, each with a name, an optional date and
        optional notes. You add them yourself: we never take them from your photos. Your places are
        <strong>private</strong>: only you can see them, and they don't appear on your profile, your blog or anywhere
        else on PhotoWorld.
    </p>
    <p>
        The map is drawn by <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org" rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap</a>, so your browser
        loads map images from their servers, which see your IP address and the area you're viewing. When you search
        for a place, or click the map and we suggest a name, what you typed or the point you clicked is sent to
        OpenStreetMap's <a href="https://nominatim.org" rel="noopener">Nominatim</a> service. Nothing about your
        account is sent. See the <a href="https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy" rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap
        Foundation privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        In the PhotoWorld app for iPhone and iPad, the map is Apple Maps instead, and place searches and suggested
        names use Apple's location services, under <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" rel="noopener">Apple's
        privacy policy</a>. In the Android app they are provided by Google Maps and Android's location services, under
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>. The apps only use
        the camera or your photo library when you choose to share a photo, and only the photo you pick is uploaded.
    </p>

    <h3>Email notifications</h3>
    <p>
        If you switch on <strong>email notifications</strong> in your account settings (or the app), we store the email
        address you choose, how often you want emails (as soon as a post is published, daily or weekly) and when we last
        emailed you. If it isn't the address your Google account shares with us, we first send it a confirmation link
        and email nothing else until you click it. We use it only to tell you about new blog posts on PhotoWorld. Every
        email has an unsubscribe link, and you can switch notifications off or change the address at any time. The
        emails are delivered by our email service provider, which processes the address and the email only to send it.
    </p>

    <h2>How we use it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>to create your account and recognise you when you sign in,</li>
        <li>to show your name and picture on your profile and next to what you share,</li>
        <li>to show your photos, likes and comments to other members,</li>
        <li>to keep the service secure, investigate problems and prevent abuse.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We don't sell your personal information, and we don't use it for advertising.</p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>PhotoWorld only uses cookies that are necessary for the site to work:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>PhotoWorld.Auth</strong>: keeps you signed in (up to 8 hours).</li>
        <li><strong>PhotoWorld.External</strong>: used for a few minutes while you sign in with Google.</li>
        <li><strong>.AspNetCore.Antiforgery</strong> and <strong>.AspNetCore.Correlation</strong>: protect forms
            and the sign-in process against forgery.</li>
        <li><strong>.AspNetCore.Mvc.CookieTempDataProvider</strong>: carries one-time messages, such as the
            welcome message after you sign in, to the next page.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We don't use analytics or advertising cookies.</p>

    <h2>Where your data is stored</h2>
    <p>
        Your account information, photos and our logs are stored by
        <a href="https://supabase.com" rel="noopener">Supabase</a> in the London (eu-west-2) region.
        Sign-in is provided by Google, under <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's
        privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Account information</strong>: for as long as you have a PhotoWorld account.</li>
        <li><strong>Photos, blog posts, places, likes and comments</strong>: until you delete them or your account.</li>
        <li><strong>Notification email address and settings</strong>: until you delete your account. Switching
            notifications off stops all emails; you can also replace the address.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical logs</strong>: 30 days, then deleted automatically.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
    <p>
        You can delete your account, with all your photos, places, notification settings, likes and comments, at any time from
        <strong>Account settings</strong> on your member page. It takes effect immediately. You can also ask us
        for a copy of the personal information we hold about you, or ask us to correct it. You can also withdraw PhotoWorld's access at any
        time from your <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections" rel="noopener">Google account
        settings</a>. If you live in the UK or EU, you can also complain to your data protection authority.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>PhotoWorld is not intended for children under 13, and we don't knowingly collect their information.</p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>If we change this policy, we'll update the date at the top of this page.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(contactEmail))
    {
        <p>Questions or requests about your data: <a href="mailto:@contactEmail">@contactEmail</a>.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="alert alert-warning">Set <code>Site:ContactEmail</code> in configuration to show a contact address here.</p>
    }
</article>
